<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derek Walcott</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930–2017</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derek Walcott was a Saint Lucian poet and playwright, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a> in 1930 when the island was a British colony. His work, including the epic poem Omeros (1990), drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> history, landscape and mixed heritage, and he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> Workshop. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derek-walcott.jpg" alt="Derek Walcott" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Nienhuis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Derek_Walcott.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
